你知道，把我们和我们最近的灵长类亲戚相比，我们通常不像其他生物那样擅长执行专门的任务。我们非常擅长即兴发挥和寻找关于我们周围世界的新思考方式。这是一个新问题。我不知道该怎么处理它。我会怎么做？这确实是我们未来需要的技能。具有讽刺意味的是，这是一种你无法通过仅仅试图学习该特定技能来学习的技能。这似乎是一种源于自由和探索感的技能，即使在其他领域也是如此。所以我认为我们可能会被诱导去思考，

48
00:24:38.000 --> 00:25:14.000
you know, compare us to our closest primate relatives, we're not actually as good at doing specialized tasks often as other creatures are. What we're very good at is improvising and finding new ways of thinking about the world around us. Here's a new problem. I don't know how to handle it. What would I do? That's really the skill that we're going to need in the future. And the irony is that that's a skill that you can't learn by just trying to learn that particular skill. That's a skill that seems to come from a sense of freedom and exploration even in other domains. So I think we may be kind of suckered into thinking,
如果我们希望儿童或成人在这个非常不可预测、多变的世界中取得成功，我们需要做的是赋予他们越来越高水平的非常具体的学术技能。我认为，即使你走到我所在的伯克利附近的硅谷，我认为他们也懂。所以，谷歌出名地给员工一天假去做他们想做的事，你知道，皮克斯（Pixar）把游戏屋作为其环境的一部分。所以我认为那种有趣探索的价值是科技界的人所理解的。但我确实认为这与这样一种感觉存在张力：这件事如此重要，必须加以塑造，而且你想要一个特定的结果。

49
00:25:14.000 --> 00:25:54.000
well, if we want children or adults to succeed in this very unpredictable, variable world, what we need to do is give them higher and higher and higher levels of very specific academic skills. And I think, you know, even if you go down to Silicon Valley near where I am at Berkeley, I think they get it. So, you know, famously Google gives their employees a day off to do whatever they want, and, you know, Pixar has playhouses as part of their environment. So I think the value of that kind of playful exploration is something that people say in the tech world get. But I do think it's intention with the sense that this is so important, it has to be shaped and you want a particular outcome.
所以讽刺的是，为了获得好的结果，有时你不专门努力去获得这些结果，而是根本不担心结果，反而能做得更好。我认为这是我在书中讨论的科学的真正深层信息之一。艾莉森·高普尼克是加州大学伯克利分校的心理学和哲学教授。

50
00:25:54.000 --> 00:26:15.000
So the irony is to get to good outcomes, sometimes you do better by not trying specifically to get to those outcomes and instead not worrying about outcomes at all. And I think that's one of the real deep messages of the science that I'm talking about in the book. Alison Gopnik is a professor of psychology and philosophy at the University of California Berkeley.
